clauses.text:
EHSS Policy shall include, as a minimum, commitments to:
a. protect people and provide healthy and safe working conditions;
b. protect the environment, including the prevention of pollution and minimization of adverse environmental impacts;
c. eliminate hazards and manage EHSS risks;
d. fulfil applicable legal requirements and other obligations;
e. consult and enable the participation of workers; and
f. continually improve EHSS performance and MSHEM.

When establishing and revising EHSS objectives, Business Units and Corporate Functions shall consider:
a. EHSS risks and opportunities;
b. applicable legal and regulatory requirements;
c. Maaden’s strategic and business priorities;
d. previous and current EHSS performance;
e. significant incidents and relevant lessons learned;
f. audit, assurance and management review findings.

EHSS objectives and EHSS objective plans shall be reviewed and, where justified, revised when necessary to address:
a. material changes to activities, assets, organization or operating conditions;
b. changes to material EHSS risks or opportunities;
c. new or changed legal requirements, regulatory requirements, permit conditions or other obligations;
d. significant incidents, findings or lessons learned;
e. inadequate progress or failure to achieve an objective;
f. changes to Maaden’s strategic or business priorities; or
g. identified opportunities to improve EHSS performance or MSHEM.

Applicable EHSS legal and regulatory requirements shall be integrated, as applicable, into:
a. EHSS risk assessments;
b. Operational controls;
c. Management of change;
d. EHSS objectives and plans;
e. Competency and training requirements.

Risk assessments shall consider, as applicable:
a. Routine and non-routine activities;
b. Normal, abnormal and emergency conditions;
c. Employees, contractors, visitors and other affected people;
d. Relevant facility, project, product and service lifecycle stages;
e. Planned and reasonably foreseeable changes; and
f. Environmental aspects, occupational health exposures, safety hazards, process hazards and security threats.
g. Existing controls and safeguards; and
h. Relevant incidents, findings, operating experience and changes in applicable obligations.

Risk assessments shall identify and evaluate:
a. The hazard, exposure, aspect, threat or risk source;
b. Credible unwanted events and consequence scenarios;
c. People, the environment, assets or operations that may be affected;
d. Potential consequences and their severity;
e. Likelihood of occurrence;
f. Existing controls and safeguards;
g. Initial risk and residual risk.

A risk assessment shall be conducted or reviewed when new EHSS risks arise or existing risks change, including:
a. facility and plant design, construction, commissioning and operation;
b. management of Change activities;
c. unresolved Pre-Start-Up Safety Review actions;
d. abnormal test, inspection or monitoring results;
e. deferred inspections, tests or maintenance activities associated with EHSS critical asset or systems;
f. bypasses, impairments or disabling of a critical control, protective device or emergency system;
g. significant findings from incidents, audits, inspections, assessments or investigations; and
h. unsafe conditions that cannot be immediately eliminated or controlled.
i. evidence that an existing control or safeguard is degraded, unavailable or ineffective.

Risk registers shall identify, as a minimum:
a. The hazard or risk source;
b. The unwanted event;
c. The risk level;
d. Existing controls and safeguards;
e. Interim controls;
f. Required mitigation actions;
g. Responsible people;
h. Target completion dates; and
i. Review and verification requirements.

The performance specification shall establish specific and verifiable performance requirements and define, as applicable:
a. the control objective and associated material unwanted event;
b. required performance and applicable operating limits;
c. factors that could degrade or compromise the control and supporting activities required to maintain its performance;
d. verification requirements and acceptance criteria;
e. performance triggers, including applicable Go/No-Go criteria and required response; and
f. the accountable Control Owner.

Business Units and Corporate Functions shall review and, where necessary, update environmental risk and impact assessments at defined intervals and whenever any of the following occurs:
a. significant change to activities, processes, products, services, facilities or operating conditions;
b. new information becomes available that may affect the assessment outcomes;
c. a significant environmental incident occurs;
d. a change to applicable legal, regulatory, permit or other compliance obligations occurs;
e. an audit, assurance, inspection or review activities identify the need for reassessment.

The environmental footprint aspect assessment shall consider, where applicable:
a. unknown reporting boundaries;
b. excluded data sources and Inaccurate source data;
c. metering equipment deficiencies and failures;
d. inconsistent methodologies and unauthorized calculation factors;
e. non verified estimates; and
f. calculation errors and Disclosure oversight.

Business Units and Corporate Functions shall complete and maintain a needs assessment for medical services, health surveillance and medical emergency response before starting applicable activities or operations and following changes that could affect these needs, considering:
a. workforce and contractor characteristics;
b. occupational health and communicable disease risks;
c. credible medical emergencies;
d. location, remoteness and response times;
e. managed accommodation;
f. external healthcare and public health capabilities;
g. operating experience; and
h. applicable obligations

Business Units and Corporate Functions shall define the activities requiring a Permit to Work and implement a Permit to Work system that includes the following permit types where applicable:
a. General Work Permit;
b. Hot Work Permit;
c. Confined Space Entry Permit;
d. Electrical Work Permit; and
e. Line and Equipment Opening Permit.

The Permit to Work system shall, as a minimum, include the following supporting certificates where applicable to the work activity:
a. Excavation Certificate;
b. LOTO Certificate;
c. Lifting Certificate;
d. Radiography Certificate;
e. Confined Space Entry Log.
f. Gas Test Record Log.

An exemption under 3.21.9 shall not apply where the task:
a. falls outside the approved scope or conditions of the applicable procedure;
b. involves permit controlled work, breaking containment, hazardous energy exposure or hazardous atmosphere;
c. creates significant interfaces with other work or operations;
d. is subject to a legal, regulatory or site requirement for a Permit to Work; Where the task cannot be performed within the approved procedure or any required control cannot be implemented or maintained, the exemption shall cease to apply and the task shall be reassessed before work starts or continues.

Permitted work shall be stopped and the permit suspended where:
a. An incident, emergency, alarm or evacuation affects the work;
b. the approved work scope, work method, work location, equipment, hazards, isolation boundaries or worksite conditions change;
c. a required control becomes unavailable or ineffective;
d. atmospheric conditions fall outside approved limits;
e. an uncontrolled interaction with another activity arises; or
f. any person identifies that the work cannot be performed safely.

A Job Safety Analysis (JSA) shall be completed by a team including competent personnel with knowledge of the tasks, hazards and required controls. A JSA shall be required where the task:
a. is high risk, complex or non-routine, including but not limited to hot work, confined space entry, working at height, lifting activities, work involving energy isolation, excavation, radiography, use of explosives and diving;
b. requires detailed step by step assessment because of the nature of the hazards or controls;
c. involves significant operational, contractor or simultaneous work interfaces;
d. is new, unfamiliar, being performed for the first time, or involves new methods, equipment or technology;
e. involves changing or uncertain conditions;
f. requires specialized or multidisciplinary knowledge.

Task Risk Assessments shall identify, as applicable:
a. the task and work conditions;
b. hazards, exposures, interactions and credible consequences;
c. people who may be affected;
d. existing and required controls;
e. applicable critical controls and verification requirements;
f. responsibilities for implementing controls; and
g. conditions requiring work to stop or the assessment to be reviewed.

Before isolation controls are removed, it shall be verified that:
a. affected work is complete;
b. personnel are clear;
c. tools, temporary equipment and materials are removed;
d. guards, protective devices and safeguards are restored; and
e. equipment is suitable for safe return to service.

Work at height shall, where reasonably practicable, be avoided or otherwise controlled in the following order:
a. fall prevention;
b. collective fall protection;
c. travel restraint;
d. work positioning; and
e. fall arrest.

Work at height shall be planned to define, as applicable:
a. the safe work method and access;
b. working surfaces and equipment;
c. collective and personal fall protection measures;
d. anchorage requirements;
e. falling object and area controls;
f. environmental operating limits;
g. interfaces with other work and applicable permits; and
h. rescue arrangements.

As a minimum, the following shall be treated as critical lifts unless a more stringent Business Unit criterion applies:
a. lifts using more than one lifting machine;
b. lifting of personnel;
c. lifts over or adjacent to occupied areas, live plant, hazardous inventories or public areas;
d. lifts within applicable approach distances of energized electrical conductors;
e. lifts above 75% of available rated capacity at the planned configuration;
f. lifts where load weight or center of gravity is not adequately verified;
g. blind lifts or lifts with restricted operator visibility; and
h. erection, dismantling or major reconfiguration of a lifting machine.

The lifting plan shall address, as applicable:
a. the load, weight, center of gravity, integrity and lifting points;
b. lifting machine, configuration, capacity and operating radius;
c. rigging arrangement;
d. equipment positioning and supporting conditions;
e. lift path and landing location;
f. exclusion zones;
g. communication and signaling;
h. weather and environmental limits;
i. electrical, process, traffic and adjacent work hazards;
j. hold points and stop-work conditions;
k. emergency or load recovery arrangements; and
l. required supervision, authorization and interfaces with other work controls.

Excavation and trenching work shall be planned and risk assessed before work begins, considering, as applicable:
a. excavation scope, dimensions and method;
b. ground and soil conditions;
c. underground and overhead services;
d. adjacent structures, foundations, roads and facilities;
e. groundwater, water ingress and flooding;
f. hazardous or contaminated atmospheres and ground;
g. access and egress;
h. equipment, vehicles and lifting activities;
i. spoil and surface loads;
j. fall and falling object hazards;
k. electrical, process, fire, explosion and environmental hazards; and
l. simultaneous operations and emergency conditions.

The assessment shall consider, as applicable:
a. current and proposed mine design and mining method;
b. credible geotechnical and ground control failure mechanisms;
c. geological, geotechnical, hydrogeological and seismic conditions;
d. excavation geometry, mine sequencing and blasting;
e. ground support systems;
f. interaction with existing workings, excavations or voids;
g. previous ground instability or geotechnical events; and
h. exposure of people, equipment and infrastructure to potential failure. The assessment shall be conducted under the direction of a qualified person with involvement of the technical disciplines necessary to understand the credible failure mechanisms and controls.

Trigger Action Response Plans shall be established for geotechnical failure mechanisms where monitoring, inspection or observed ground behavior can indicate deteriorating conditions, and define:
a. engineering based trigger levels;
b. required actions for each trigger level;
c. stop-work, access restriction and evacuation criteria, where applicable;
d. responsible people and escalation requirements;
e. communication arrangements; and
f. interfaces with applicable emergency-response arrangements.

Each underground mine shall establish and maintain a Ventilation Plan approved by an appropriately qualified ventilation engineer that defines:
a. the ventilation design basis and required airflow distribution;
b. primary and auxiliary ventilation arrangements;
c. atmospheric monitoring and exposure controls;
d. ventilation system critical controls and TARPs; and
e. emergency ventilation arrangements.

Ventilation circuits, fans, ducting and control devices shall be designed, installed, operated, inspected and maintained to provide capacity for foreseeable personnel, equipment, production and contaminant loads, and to:
a. provide controlled airflow to occupied areas;
b. prevent unacceptable recirculation and contamination of fresh air circuits;
c. maintain required circuit separation and system integrity; and
d. protect escapeways and refuge locations from foreseeable smoke and hazardous-gas migration.

Refuge chambers shall:
a. protect occupants from credible underground emergency conditions;
b. provide the required life-support capacity and duration;
c. remain accessible during foreseeable emergencies;
d. have reliable emergency power, monitoring and two-way communication; and
e. be commissioned, inspected, tested and maintained to demonstrate readiness.

Re-entry to an underground area affected by fire, explosion, smoke, hazardous atmosphere, ventilation failure or another emergency shall only occur after competent personnel have:
a. assessed the affected area;
b. verified that conditions are safe for the intended activity;
c. confirmed the availability of required controls, monitoring, communications and emergency arrangements; and
d. authorized entry in accordance with applicable TARPs and emergency-response arrangements.

The assessment shall address, where applicable, hazards, initiating events, failure modes, and potential consequences associated with:
a. contact with water, moisture, or wet materials, tools, or equipment;
b. incompatible, contaminated, reactive, or enclosed charge materials;
c. splashing, foaming, overflow, leakage, run-out, breakout, or loss of containment;
d. failure of refractory, cooling, structural, lifting, transfer, or containment systems;
e. loss or disturbance of power, cooling, fuel, air, hydraulic, or control systems;
f. uncontrolled movement, collision, derailment, rollover, or dropped loads;
g. exposure to heat, flame, fumes, gases, dust, noise, or projected material;
h. electrical or electromagnetic hazards;
i. restricted access, egress, or emergency escape; and
j. resulting fire, explosion, injury, exposure, or asset damage.

The design basis shall define:
a. the safe operating envelope and applicable material compatibility requirements;
b. design criteria for containment, diversion, and release management;
c. cooling and utility requirements, including the required response to interruption and restoration;
d. alarms, trips, shutdown functions, and emergency controls, including the need for automatic, local, or remote operation as determined by the hazard assessment; and
e. access control, shielding, emergency escape, and safe refuge requirements.

Warehouse operating limits shall be defined, documented, communicated and verified for compliance, and address, as applicable:
a. permitted materials, commodity classifications and maximum quantities;
b. permitted storage heights and configurations;
c. floor, mezzanine and storage-system capacities;
d. fire-protection design assumptions and limitations;
e. material-handling equipment and methods;
f. traffic arrangements; and
g. loading, unloading and adjacent operational interfaces.

As a minimum, the drill and exercise program shall:
a. include muster, evacuation, tabletop, and emergency team response exercises at least quarterly, conducted individually or in combination;
b. include periodic area-, unit-, and shift-specific drills addressing the credible scenarios applicable to them;
c. cover the full range of prioritized credible scenarios within a cycle not exceeding two years; and
d. include at least one large-scale drill annually involving the full emergency organization and applicable mutual aid parties, with the required advance notification provided to SAIS.
